<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gloria in excelsis Deo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd or 3rd century; Latin translation 4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Gloria in excelsis Deo&quot; (&quot;Glory to God in the highest&quot;) is a Christian hymn also known as the Greater Doxology, the Angelic Hymn or the Hymn of the Angels. It opens with the words the angels sing to the shepherds at the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, and other verses were added very early to make a hymn of praise. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Flusser linked the verse in Luke with ancient Jewish liturgy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Gloria in excelsis Deo</b>&quot; (&quot;Glory to God in the highest&quot;) is a Christian hymn also known as the Greater Doxology, the Angelic Hymn or the Hymn of the Angels.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li></ul></section>
